**Input:** a plan in `docs/local/plans/` with `Status: COMPLETE`. **Output:** `Status: VERIFIED` with evidence for every check — or fix tasks added and a loop back to `spec-implement`. The loop is automatic: plan approval was the only user checkpoint, so never ask whether to fix.

## Phase 1 — Review (once per chain)

**One review, one fix commit, no re-review.** `review-diff` runs once per chain, over `<Base>...HEAD`. A fix commit is never sent back through `review-diff`: a fresh high-recall pass always surfaces new suggestions, so re-reviewing fixes never converges. Fix commits get the *fix check* below instead.

1. **Plan audit** (first — cheap, and before the model switches to Fable for the review) — every task ticked, Done/Left agree, **every doc the chain touched is refreshed** (doc comments on changed symbols, Why/What docs on new and edited tests, READMEs / docs pages / CLAUDE.md / ADRs / generated references — a stale or missing doc is `must_fix`, and each `Docs-Impact: none` claim is checked against its diff), each story has exactly one commit with its SHA recorded, no undocumented deviation, every **Goal Verification** truth holds and every artifact exists and is non-stub, every *Guards* check the plan names exists and fails on a seeded violation (a conformance claim counts only with a check's output — never on the model's own reading of the rule). Audit each `Trivial:` claim against the diff (the `testing.md` limits); a failed claim is `must_fix` (remove it, write the RED test).
2. **Code review** — `Skill(skill='review-diff', args='<Base>...HEAD plan=<plan path>')`, skipped when `Reviewed:` is already set. It runs as a foreground fork on Fable — the diff never enters this context; only the findings come back. It covers tests, doubles, and parsimony — don't re-audit them here. Set `Reviewed:` to `git rev-parse HEAD` and copy every finding (tier + `file:line`) into the plan's `## Review Findings` — the fix checklist.
3. **Bugfix — Behavior Contract** — the exact trigger now produces the correct behavior; every parallel implementation the plan names was fixed.

**Every tier is fixed by default** — `must_fix`, `should_fix`, and `suggestion` alike, all in one fix commit. Skip a finding only when it is wrong for this codebase (`code-review-reception.md` push-back), with the reason recorded next to it. Any finding open → loop back now (Phase 5 *Otherwise*); don't prove, execute, or gate code that is about to change.

**Fix check (every return from a loop-back, instead of a review)** — inline, no agent: read `git diff <Reviewed>...HEAD`; every recorded finding is resolved or has a recorded skip; the fix touches nothing beyond them; spec-implement's fast checks were green. An unresolved finding, or a `must_fix`-grade defect the fix itself introduced → one more fix task. Don't hunt for new findings. `Iteration` past 3 → stop and ask the user; the loop is not converging.

## Phase 2 — Bugfix: revert-test proof

NEVER mark a bugfix VERIFIED without it. Revert the fix (not the test) with Edit — `git show <Base>:<path>` shows the pre-fix lines — and run the reproducing test: it MUST fail. Re-apply with Edit: it MUST pass and `git diff` is empty again. Never `git stash` / `git checkout` for this. A test that passes with the fix reverted doesn't pin the bug — rewrite it. On a loop-back, re-prove only if the fix touched the fix lines or the reproducing test.

## Phase 3 — Execute (once, before the gate)

Tests passing ≠ the program working. NEVER skip: run the real CLI / call the API / drive the UI (Read `~/.claude/rules/browser-automation.md` and run its live-target probe) — for a bugfix, on the **original trigger**. It runs before the full gate so any fix it forces lands before the one gate run. A failure → fix tasks, loop back (fix check, not review), then execute once more.

## Phase 4 — Full gate (once, last)

Runs exactly once, after the review cycle has closed — every finding resolved, revert-test proven, execution passed — so nothing left can change the code. NEVER run it earlier: not per story, not to check a fix, not "to be safe". The tree must be clean (a project gate may check it): `git status --short` shows nothing but the gitignored plan. Unrelated files the user chose to keep at the start → ask the user to commit or stash them (never do it yourself), then run. Skip if `Full gate:` already records HEAD. Green → `Full gate: green @ <sha>`. Red → fix it without re-running the gate to iterate: re-run only the failing step's own command (the one linter / suite / check the gate reported) until it's green, commit the fix (loop back; fix check only, no review), then run the full gate once more. Flag any production file over 800 lines (generated files exempt).

## Phase 5 — Decide

**Every finding resolved — all three tiers, or skipped with a recorded reason — everything committed, full gate green at HEAD:** set `Status: VERIFIED` and report each check with its evidence. Report the **Human review** list — the plan's, plus any changed file touching auth, permissions, migrations, public contracts, or infrastructure, and the first instance of a new pattern: those a human reads in full; the rest is sampled. Then close every story the plan covers (the `story` layout folder, `~/.claude/templates/docs-layout.md`) (`**Status**: Complete`, plus its row in the epic) and commit those edits as `docs(spec): close story <ids>` — chain-authorized; docs-only, so it inherits the gate (`Full gate: green @ <sha> (+docs <sha2>)`). Where the project tracks work in an issue tracker (`linear.md`), update the ticket state through its authenticated MCP if wired — otherwise say what to update. **VERIFIED is merged-ready, not done:** when the plan's Operational surface ≥ 2 or its class is Gate and soak, print the epic's *Soak* checklist — the epic stays open until `**Stable**` is set after staging.

**Otherwise:** add fix tasks to the plan (and to Left), set `Status: PENDING`, keep `Approved: Yes`, increment `Iteration`, and call `Skill(skill='spec-implement')` in the same turn. NEVER mark VERIFIED with an open finding.

## Next Step

Suggest `/github pr` (user-typed) to push the commits and open a PR — no full-gate re-run. The tree is clean: every commit the chain made is listed with its SHA. Long session → suggest `/compact` (or `/clear` when the next work is unrelated) before starting anything else.
